Theorem PL-6: adding a pass requires updating ordering + proving no constraint violation — the meta-theorem governing pipeline extension

  Preconditions:
    - The AIMS pipeline is a 12-step strictly-ordered sequence per
      Annex E §AIMS §6. PL-6 is the META-RULE that
      governs how the schedule itself may be EXTENDED: it does not fix a
      single ordered pair (the way PL-2/PL-3/PL-4/PL-4a do) but constrains
      every act of adding a new pass to the schedule.
    - PL-6 statement (per Annex E §AIMS PL-6 + Annex E §AIMS §6
      "Do NOT add passes without updating the pipeline. Do NOT call out of
      order."): adding a pass requires (1) updating the pipeline ordering to
      place the new pass at a declared position, (2) proving the insertion
      violates no existing ordering constraint, and (3) a matched commit pair
      touching BOTH the pipeline AND its tests.
    - PL-6 type signature (per Pipeline.proof lines 54-57, the sorry
      obligation this theorem discharges):
        type : NewPass -> List Constraint
        adding a pass requires updating ordering + proving no constraint
        violation; matched commit pair touching both pipeline + tests.
    - The existing ordering constraints the meta-rule must preserve are the
      shipped §07.1 + §07.2 ordered pairs over the per-function suffix
      schedule (Steps 3..12 with sub-steps 3a/4a/5a/8a):
        PL-1 Steps 1-2 (interprocedural prefix) before any per-function step
        PL-2 Step 4 (analyze_function) < Step 5 (realize_rc_reuse)
        PL-3 Step 5 (realize_rc_reuse) < Step 9 (merge_blocks)
        PL-4 Step 9 (merge_blocks) < Step 10 (realize_annotations)
        PL-4a Step 8a (unwind_cleanup) < Step 9 (merge_blocks)
      A NewPass insertion is VALID iff, after insertion, every one of these
      ordered-pair predicates still holds AND the NewPass's own declared
      ordering dependencies (which existing steps it must follow / precede)
      are satisfied by its declared position.
    - Shipped grounding for the meta-rule's enforcement surface:
        (a) The pipeline ordering is encoded as the straight-line statement
            sequence of ori_arc::pipeline::aims_pipeline::run_aims_pipeline
            (compiler/ori_arc/src/pipeline/aims_pipeline/mod.rs
            lines 163-287) + postprocess::verify_and_merge
            (compiler/ori_arc/src/pipeline/aims_pipeline/
            postprocess.rs lines 15-51). Inserting a pass = adding a statement
            at a chosen position in this sequence; "updating ordering" is
            literally editing this driver.
        (b) The "Do NOT add passes without updating the pipeline. Do NOT call
            out of order." directive (Annex E §AIMS §6
            sec-Pipeline-ordering, the last bullet) is the human-readable
            statement of the meta-rule.
        (c) The "matched commit pair touching both pipeline + tests" leg
            mirrors the project-wide PL-6 discipline (Pipeline.proof line 57):
            a pipeline-ordering edit lands with its ordering tests in the same
            commit, so a constraint regression is caught at commit time.
            Annex E §AIMS §6 (relationship to §8) ("PL-6 governs in-pipeline
            passes; post-pipeline ops on output") fixes the SCOPE of the
            meta-rule to in-pipeline insertions.
    - This theorem reasons over the SET of ordering constraints the schedule
      must satisfy AFTER a hypothetical NewPass insertion. It is downstream of
      PL-2/PL-3/PL-4/PL-4a (the constraints it must preserve) and is the
      meta-level companion of PL-5 (a pass added out of order would read a
      stale or absent summary — a PL-5 violation the PL-6 constraint-check
      prevents at insertion time).

  Soundness property:
    Forall NewPass p with a declared position pos(p) in the per-function
    schedule and a declared dependency set deps(p) = (must_follow(p),
    must_precede(p)) over the existing attributed steps.
      Let SCHED' = SCHED with p inserted at pos(p).
      Three conjuncts SHALL hold for p to be an ADMISSIBLE insertion:

      (P1) Ordering-updated:
        p carries a declared position pos(p) in SCHED' (the insertion is not
        "called out of order" — it occupies an explicit ordinal, not an
        ad-hoc call site). Equivalently: SCHED' is a total order in which p
        has a unique position.

      (P2) No-existing-constraint-violation:
        Every existing ordered-pair predicate still holds in SCHED':
          position'(Step 4) < position'(Step 5) (PL-2)
          position'(Step 5) < position'(Step 9) (PL-3)
          position'(Step 9) < position'(Step 10) (PL-4)
          position'(Step 8a) < position'(Step 9) (PL-4a)
        AND p's own declared dependencies are satisfied:
          forall s in must_follow(p). position'(s) < pos(p)
          forall s in must_precede(p). pos(p) < position'(s)

      (P3) Matched-commit-pair:
        The insertion is accompanied by a test update in the same change set
        (the ordering tests that pin the preserved predicates). Modeled as:
        p declares `tests_updated = true`; an insertion claiming admissibility
        without the matched test leg is rejected.

  Proof obligation:
    Three-part constructive discharge corresponding to (P1), (P2), (P3), by
    structural induction over the post-insertion schedule SCHED' + case
    analysis on whether the NewPass's declared position + dependencies +
    test-leg are well-formed.

    Part (P1) — Ordering-updated (structural induction over SCHED'):

      Base case — empty insertion:
        With no NewPass, SCHED' = SCHED is the shipped total order
        (pl_suffix_schedule's monotone positions). PL-2/PL-3/PL-4/PL-4a all
        hold (discharged by §07.2). The schedule is admissible.

      Inductive step — single NewPass insertion at pos(p):
        Inserting p at pos(p) and renumbering the suffix positions yields
        SCHED' in which every original step retains its relative order and p
        occupies a unique ordinal. The insertion is "updating the ordering"
        (editing the run_aims_pipeline straight-line sequence, mod.rs lines
        163-287) — NOT an out-of-order ad-hoc call. (P1) holds: p has a
        declared position.

    Part (P2) — No-existing-constraint-violation (case analysis on pos(p)
    relative to each preserved pair):

      Case pos(p) outside an ordered pair's span (before both endpoints or
      after both):
        Inserting p before Step 4 or after Step 10 shifts both endpoints of
        every pair by the same offset; relative order is preserved, so every
        predicate position'(before) < position'(after) still holds.

      Case pos(p) strictly between an ordered pair's endpoints (e.g. between
      Step 4 and Step 5):
        Inserting p between Step 4 and Step 5 keeps position'(Step 4) < pos(p)
        < position'(Step 5), so PL-2 still holds; the other pairs (whose spans
        do not contain pos(p), or contain it on the same side of both
        endpoints) are likewise preserved. The verifier re-checks ALL four
        predicates on SCHED' and accepts only when every one holds.

      Case pos(p) violating a preserved pair (e.g. inserting a pass that
      MOVES realize_annotations before merge_blocks):
        Such an insertion makes position'(Step 9) > position'(Step 10),
        violating PL-4. The verifier's re-check of the PL-4 predicate Fails;
        the insertion is REJECTED. This is the constructive heart of the
        meta-rule — an admissible insertion is exactly one whose SCHED'
        satisfies every preserved predicate.

      Case p's own dependencies unsatisfied (must_follow / must_precede):
        If p declares it must follow Step 4 (it consumes the AimsStateMap)
        but is placed before Step 4, position'(Step 4) < pos(p) is false; the
        verifier Fails. p is rejected — placing it there would read an absent
        state map (a PL-5 violation). Symmetrically for must_precede.

      In every admissible case the verifier confirms ALL preserved predicates
      hold AND p's declared dependencies are satisfied; in every inadmissible
      case it Fails. (P2) discharged.

    Part (P3) — Matched-commit-pair (case analysis on the test leg):

      Case tests_updated true:
        The insertion declares its ordering tests updated in the same change
        set — the matched commit pair (pipeline edit + ordering-test edit)
        exists. The verifier accepts the test leg.

      Case tests_updated false:
        The insertion claims admissibility without the matched test leg — a
        pipeline-ordering change with no test pinning the preserved
        predicates. Per Annex E §AIMS PL-6 + Pipeline.proof line 57 the
        matched commit pair is REQUIRED; the verifier Fails. (P3) discharged.

    Coverage gate — admissibility-case enumeration:

      The verifier models a representative set of NewPass insertions against
      the shipped pl_suffix_schedule and discharges, for each, the triple
      (P1 ordering-updated, P2 no-constraint-violation, P3 matched-commit-
      pair). It asserts:
        - an admissible insertion (declared position + dependencies satisfied
          + tests updated) is ACCEPTED, with every preserved predicate
          (PL-2/PL-3/PL-4/PL-4a) re-verified to hold on SCHED'.
        - an insertion that violates a preserved predicate is REJECTED.
        - an insertion that leaves a declared dependency unsatisfied is
          REJECTED.
        - an insertion lacking the matched test leg is REJECTED.

      Coverage axes:
        Ordering-updated (P1): the admissible-insertion case verified to
          occupy a unique declared position in SCHED'.
        No-constraint-violation (P2): the four preserved ordered-pair
          predicates re-verified on SCHED' for the admissible case + the
          predicate-violating and dependency-violating cases rejected.
        Matched-commit-pair (P3): the tests-updated case accepted, the
          tests-missing case rejected.

    Negative-direction witness — out-of-order insertion rejected:

      An insertion that places a state-map consumer before Step 4
      analyze_function (the "Do NOT call out of order" prohibition, Annex E §AIMS
      sec-Critical-Rules) leaves position'(Step 4) < pos(p) false; the
      verifier Fails the dependency check. The pass would read an absent
      AimsStateMap — a PL-5 staleness/absence bug. The meta-rule rejects it
      at insertion time.

    Negative-direction witness — constraint-violating insertion rejected:

      An insertion that reorders Step 10 realize_annotations ahead of Step 9
      merge_blocks makes position'(Step 9) > position'(Step 10), violating
      PL-4 (phase 2 would read position-keyed maps invalidated by merge — see
      PL-3/PL-4). The verifier re-checks PL-4 on SCHED' and Fails. The
      meta-rule rejects any insertion that breaks a preserved predicate.

    Negative-direction witness — test-less insertion rejected:

      An insertion claiming admissibility with tests_updated = false is a
      pipeline edit with no ordering test pinning the preserved predicates —
      a regression would slip past commit. Per PL-6's matched-commit-pair leg
      the verifier Fails. The meta-rule requires the test leg.

    Cycle-soundness witness — PL-6 is the meta-companion of the ordered pairs
    + PL-5:

      PL-6 governs SCHEDULE EXTENSION; the ordered-pair rules PL-2/PL-3/PL-4/
      PL-4a are the constraints it preserves, and PL-5 is the staleness
      invariant it protects (an out-of-order insertion would read a stale or
      absent summary). PL-6 consumes the ordered-pair predicates as its
      "preserved set"; it produces no predicate they consume. The dependency
      chain is acyclic:
        PL-2 + PL-3 + PL-4 + PL-4a --> PL-6 (the preserved constraints an
                                            insertion must not violate)
        PL-5 --> PL-6 (the staleness invariant an
                                            out-of-order insertion would break)
      PL-6 is a sink of this dependency tree.

    Algorithm-citation declaration:
      property: pipeline-extension admissibility — a new pass is
                           admissible iff it occupies a declared position,
                           violates no existing ordering constraint, and lands
                           with a matched ordering-test commit
      published: standard pass-manager extension discipline — adding
                           a pass to an optimization pipeline requires
                           declaring its position + its pass dependencies +
                           preserving the analysis-validity constraints of
                           existing passes; the same shape governs LLVM's
                           PassManager pass-dependency / analysis-preservation
                           declarations from the published reference-compiler corpus
      determinism: deterministic — admissibility of a given NewPass
                           insertion is a fixed function of its declared
                           position, its declared dependency set, and the
                           fixed pl_suffix_schedule's preserved predicates;
                           no schedule freedom beyond the declared position
      reference impl: ori_arc::pipeline::aims_pipeline::run_aims_pipeline
                           (mod.rs lines 163-287, the straight-line statement
                           sequence that IS the ordering an insertion edits) +
                           postprocess::verify_and_merge (postprocess.rs lines
                           15-51) + the "Do NOT add passes without updating the
                           pipeline. Do NOT call out of order." directive
                           (Annex E §AIMS §6)

    Engines dispatched:
      structural_induction (PRIMARY — structural induction over the
        post-insertion schedule SCHED' per section_07::
        verify_pl6_adding_a_pass_meta_rule; the verifier models a NewPass with
        declared position + dependency set + test leg, builds SCHED', and
        discharges (P1) ordering-updated via the unique-position check, (P2)
        no-constraint-violation via re-checking the four preserved ordered-pair
        predicates PL-2/PL-3/PL-4/PL-4a on SCHED' PLUS the NewPass's declared
        dependencies, and (P3) matched-commit-pair via the test-leg flag;
        emits Fail on a predicate violation, an unsatisfied dependency, or a
        missing test leg)
      case_analysis (SECONDARY — per-case grid over the admissible vs
        predicate-violating vs dependency-violating vs test-less insertion
        cases; gracious-accept once structural_induction has discharged the
        meta-rule obligation per the coverage-manifest PL row)

  Expected:
    status: valid
    reason: section_07 structural_induction engine discharges PL-6 via the post-insertion schedule SCHED' — (P1) an admissible NewPass occupies a unique declared position (ordering updated, not called out of order), (P2) every preserved ordered-pair predicate PL-2/PL-3/PL-4/PL-4a still holds on SCHED' AND the NewPass's declared dependencies are satisfied (a predicate-violating or dependency-violating insertion is rejected), and (P3) the insertion lands with a matched ordering-test commit (a test-less insertion is rejected); grounded in the shipped run_aims_pipeline straight-line ordering (mod.rs lines 163-287) + the "Do NOT add passes without updating the pipeline. Do NOT call out of order." directive (Annex E §AIMS §6); PL-6 is the meta-companion of PL-5 (an out-of-order insertion would read a stale/absent summary); case_analysis secondary-accepting per the coverage-manifest PL row
